+## Amending a local model
+
+Promote and demote used to be the whole story, which made the layer
+**create-and-delete only**: changing one field meant demoting and re-promoting
+the same entry. Two subcommands close that, and both edit `registry.local.json`
+and nothing else:
+
+```
+bash scripts/catalog.sh rename --slug <engine>/<old> --to <engine>/<new>
+bash scripts/catalog.sh update --slug <engine>/<name> --set workload=fast-chat --set max_ctx=8192
+```
+
+The front door is `scripts/catalog.sh`; the executor is
+`scripts/lib/profiles/amend.py` (same split as promote/demote). Both take
+`--dry-run` (print the plan, write nothing) and `--root <dir>` (a throwaway tree).
+
+`rename` re-keys the registry entry **and moves no file** — the model id, its
+`models.d/<id>.yml` profile and its compose tree stay where they are, because the
+registry key is the slug and the files are keyed by model id. It exists for the
+shadow case (#1204): a local slug that collides with a newly-shipped curated one
+is kept and marked, and renaming is how you get it back.
+
+`update --set KEY=VALUE` is repeatable; each value is read as JSON when it parses
+(`tp=2`, `mem_util=0.9`, `drafter=null`) and kept as plain text otherwise.
+
+Both refuse (exit 3, nothing written) a slug that is not in this layer — naming
+it as CURATED when it is one — and:
+
+- a rename onto a slug that already exists locally, or that is a **curated** one
+  (that would re-create the shadow it just cured);
+- a rename whose engine namespace (the part before `/`) is not a known engine —
+  the namespace is what launchers derive the engine from;
+- `--set origin=…`: provenance is the loader's, never self-declared;
+- `--set engine=…`: the engine field IS the slug's namespace; `rename` moves both
+  together;
+- any `--set` of a field `_entry()` does not accept, or a value the loader would
+  reject (a bad status, a `compose_path` that does not exist).
+
+One invariant amend.py maintains: **every local row's `engine` field equals its
+slug's namespace.** The namespace is the part the launcher's engine derivation
+reads, so an entry whose `engine` disagrees with its own slug misroutes. An amend
+repairs the rows that disagree — a no-op on a layer that was already consistent.
+
